WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.589
Students often try to classify waves by how the picture looks on the page.

2
00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:09.177
That is not reliable enough.

3
00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:13.766
A wave is called transverse or longitudinal by comparing two directions.

4
00:00:13.766 --> 00:00:18.354
Direction one is the direction the wave or disturbance travels.

5
00:00:18.354 --> 00:00:22.943
Direction two is the local motion of the medium.

6
00:00:22.943 --> 00:00:27.531
If the local motion is perpendicular to the travel direction, the wave is transverse.

7
00:00:27.531 --> 00:00:32.120
If the local motion is parallel to the travel direction, the wave is longitudinal.

8
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:36.709
That is the full rule.

9
00:00:36.709 --> 00:00:41.297
The rest of the lesson is really about learning to keep those two directions visible at the same time.

10
00:00:41.297 --> 00:00:45.886
Keep this takeaway in view: Wave type comes from comparing local motion with wave travel.

11
00:00:45.886 --> 00:00:50.474
Direction one means propagation.

12
00:00:50.474 --> 00:00:55.063
Track where the wavefront or disturbance pattern is moving.

13
00:00:55.063 --> 00:00:59.651
Direction two means local motion.

14
00:00:59.651 --> 00:01:04.240
Track how the medium itself is moving in response.

15
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.978
This diagram is strong because it keeps the propagation arrow fixed while the medium response changes.

16
00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:13.716
In the cross-sway version, the medium moves at right angles to the travel direction.

17
00:01:13.716 --> 00:01:18.455
That is transverse.

18
00:01:18.455 --> 00:01:23.193
In the push-squeeze version, the medium moves along the same line as the travel direction.

19
00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:27.931
That is longitudinal.

20
00:01:27.931 --> 00:01:32.669
Notice how the travel direction does not need to change for the classification to change.

21
00:01:32.669 --> 00:01:37.407
What matters is the relationship between the two directions.

22
00:01:37.407 --> 00:01:42.145
That is the big idea the example will depend on.

23
00:01:42.145 --> 00:01:46.884
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the travel direction visible before naming the wave type.

24
00:01:46.884 --> 00:01:51.622
Transverse: Local motion is perpendicular to the direction of travel.

25
00:01:51.622 --> 00:01:56.360
Longitudinal: Local motion is parallel to the direction of travel.

26
00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.700
Imagine two lines of students passing a motion cue along a hallway.

27
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.040
In one line, each student raises and lowers an arm when the cue reaches them.

28
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.380
The signal still travels along the hallway even though the local motion is up and down.

29
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:17.720
That behaves like a transverse pattern.

30
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.060
In the second line, each student steps forward and back along the hallway as the cue reaches them.

31
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.400
Now the local motion is along the same line as the signal travel.

32
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.740
That behaves like a longitudinal pattern.

33
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:39.080
The analogy works because it makes the classification depend on comparison rather than on the visual style of the diagram.

34
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:44.420
Keep this takeaway in view: A traveling signal can be carried by different local motion directions.

35
00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.760
Sideways response means transverse pattern.

36
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.100
The signal travels one way while the local motion is at right angles.

37
00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.440
Forward-back response means longitudinal pattern.

38
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:05.780
The signal travels and the local motion stay along the same line.

39
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:11.077
The definitions are short, but every word matters.

40
00:03:11.077 --> 00:03:16.374
A transverse wave is one in which the local motion of the medium is perpendicular to the direction of propagation.

41
00:03:16.374 --> 00:03:21.671
A longitudinal wave is one in which the local motion of the medium is parallel to the direction of propagation.

42
00:03:21.671 --> 00:03:26.968
Those are not decorative textbook sentences.

43
00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:32.265
They are practical rules for classification.

44
00:03:32.265 --> 00:03:37.562
In the example, you should not ask whether the wave looks vertical or horizontal on the page.

45
00:03:37.562 --> 00:03:42.858
You should ask what direction the wave travels and what direction the local motion takes.

46
00:03:42.858 --> 00:03:48.155
Keep this takeaway in view: Accurate wave classification depends on comparing two directions in the same picture.

47
00:03:48.155 --> 00:03:53.452
Travel: Mark the propagation direction first.

48
00:03:53.452 --> 00:03:58.749
Response: Then mark the direction of local motion.

49
00:03:58.749 --> 00:04:04.046
transverse wave -> local motion perpendicular to wave travel.

50
00:04:04.046 --> 00:04:09.343
longitudinal wave -> local motion parallel to wave travel.

51
00:04:09.343 --> 00:04:14.640
A reliable answer names both directions before giving the label.

52
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.063
Here are the traps to avoid.

53
00:04:19.063 --> 00:04:23.486
First, do not call a wave transverse just because the drawing looks wavy.

54
00:04:23.486 --> 00:04:27.909
Second, do not call a wave longitudinal just because the picture shows compressions.

55
00:04:27.909 --> 00:04:32.331
Third, do not ignore the actual propagation direction if the diagram is rotated or tilted.

56
00:04:32.331 --> 00:04:36.754
The strongest habit is simple.

57
00:04:36.754 --> 00:04:41.177
State the wave travel direction out loud.

58
00:04:41.177 --> 00:04:45.600
State the local motion direction out loud.

59
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.023
Then compare them.

60
00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:54.446
That method works even when the diagram is unusual because it is based on physics, not on drawing style.

61
00:04:54.446 --> 00:04:58.869
Keep this takeaway in view: Wave type becomes easy when both directions are named before the label.

62
00:04:58.869 --> 00:05:03.291
Trap one means page appearance.

63
00:05:03.291 --> 00:05:07.714
The sketch style alone cannot classify the wave.

64
00:05:07.714 --> 00:05:12.137
Trap two means missing direction.

65
00:05:12.137 --> 00:05:16.560
A label without stating the travel direction is often incomplete.

66
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.088
Try three checks.

67
00:05:21.088 --> 00:05:25.615
If a wave travels right while particles move up and down, what is the type?

68
00:05:25.615 --> 00:05:30.143
Transverse, because the directions are perpendicular.

69
00:05:30.143 --> 00:05:34.671
If the wave travels right while particles compress and spread along the same line, what is the type?

70
00:05:34.671 --> 00:05:39.198
Longitudinal, because the directions are parallel.

71
00:05:39.198 --> 00:05:43.726
And what should you do before giving either label?

72
00:05:43.726 --> 00:05:48.254
Name the travel direction and the local motion direction.

73
00:05:48.254 --> 00:05:52.782
That is the whole lesson routine.

74
00:05:52.782 --> 00:05:57.309
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example can now be classified from direction comparison rather than memory alone.

75
00:05:57.309 --> 00:06:01.837
Right plus up-down means transverse.

76
00:06:01.837 --> 00:06:06.365
Perpendicular local motion gives the transverse label.

77
00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:10.892
Right plus push-squeeze means longitudinal.

78
00:06:10.892 --> 00:06:15.420
Parallel local motion gives the longitudinal label.

79
00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:20.727
You are ready for the worked example now.

80
00:06:20.727 --> 00:06:26.034
Start by identifying the direction the disturbance travels.

81
00:06:26.034 --> 00:06:31.341
Then identify how the medium itself moves locally.

82
00:06:31.341 --> 00:06:36.648
If the local motion is perpendicular to the travel direction, classify the wave as transverse.

83
00:06:36.648 --> 00:06:41.955
If it is parallel, classify it as longitudinal.

84
00:06:41.955 --> 00:06:47.262
If the diagram is tilted, rotated, or drawn in an unusual way, ignore the cosmetic layout and return to the same rule.

85
00:06:47.262 --> 00:06:52.568
The correct label comes from the comparison, not from the artwork.

86
00:06:52.568 --> 00:06:57.875
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: compare directions first, then classify the wave.

87
00:06:57.875 --> 00:07:03.182
Step 1: Find the direction of propagation.

88
00:07:03.182 --> 00:07:08.489
Step 2: Describe the local motion of the medium.

89
00:07:08.489 --> 00:07:13.796
mark the wave travel direction.

90
00:07:13.796 --> 00:07:19.103
mark the local motion direction.

91
00:07:19.103 --> 00:07:24.410
The worked example becomes easier when the two directions are turned into separate questions first.
